THE DRIFTWOOD DINER
Game Design Document & Implementation Plan
Year 12 Game Development  •  Godot 4  •  Solo Project
Part A — Game Design Document
1. Game Summary
The Driftwood Diner is a first-person cosy mystery game set on a fog-covered island under military occupation. The player washes ashore with no memory and takes shelter in an abandoned restaurant. The port is controlled by soldiers — passage off the island costs money. By cooking meals and serving island residents, the player earns income and trust, uncovers the island’s history through NPC stories and corkboard items, and must ultimately decide: save enough to pay for passage and leave, or choose to stay.
Engine: Godot 4  |  Perspective: First-person  |  Genre: Cosy mystery / cooking  |  Playtime: 30–60 min

2. Setting
A small fog-covered island. The diner is the only playable 3D space. The wider world is implied — glimpsed through large restaurant windows or described in NPC dialogue, photographs, and corkboard notes. The military presence at the port is a known, visible fact of island life; NPCs reference it openly. It is not a twist — it is the reason leaving costs anything at all.

3. Player Controls
•	WASD — move around the diner
•	Mouse — look around (first-person camera)
•	E — pick up an item left on the counter by an NPC
•	C — open the cooking UI overlay
•	Tab — open the recipe log
•	R — examine a corkboard item up close
•	Esc — close any open UI

4. Core Mechanic — Discovery Cooking (2D UI)
When the player opens the cooking UI, a 2D overlay appears on screen showing a virtual counter surface. The player drags ingredient icons onto the counter and combines two items to attempt a recipe. Successful combinations produce a dish or a new ingredient. Failed combinations produce nothing. There is no recipe book — discovery is the reward.
•	~30 base ingredients available as icons in a pantry panel within the UI
•	120+ combinations including chained recipes (A+B=C, then C+D=E)
•	Some recipes require leaving items on the counter overnight before completing
•	Items left out for 4+ nights go stale
•	A recipe log panel tracks all discoveries; undiscovered recipes show as ???

5. Object Interaction
The player’s only direct 3D interaction is picking up items that NPCs leave on the physical counter during their visits. These are the sole 3D interactable objects in the world. Examples include ingredients they bring from home, corkboard items (photographs, notes, clippings), and occasional story objects. Picked-up ingredients are added to the cooking UI ingredient pool. Picked-up corkboard items can be pinned to the corkboard.

6. NPC System
During the night phase, NPCs visit the diner on their own schedule. The player cooks dishes in the UI and serves them. Serving a positive dish increases a relationship meter, earns payment, and triggers new dialogue. Ignoring NPCs causes the meter to degrade — if it hits zero they stop visiting. Some NPCs pay more generously than others; some reward the player with rare ingredients instead of money.
•	~17–20 NPCs total: 2–3 deep arc, 8–10 medium arc, 6–8 light arc
•	No dialogue choices — the player listens as NPCs reveal story over repeated visits
•	Each NPC has dish affinities — certain meals earn stronger reactions and better payment
•	Some NPCs only appear under specific conditions (fog, rain, story milestones)
•	NPCs occasionally leave items on the counter when they arrive or depart

7. Economy — Paying for Passage
Leaving the island costs a fixed sum of money, controlled by the military at the port. The player earns money by serving meals to NPCs — better dishes and warmer relationships earn more. Certain late-game NPC arcs also grant lump-sum rewards or reduce the passage cost through story events. The player can check their current savings and the passage cost at any time via a UI panel.
•	Ending A (Leave): reach the passage cost threshold and choose to pay
•	Ending B (Stay): when the threshold is reached, choose not to pay — or never pursue it
•	Neither ending is framed as correct

8. Win and Lose Conditions
Condition	Type	How to reach it
Ending A — Leave	Win	Accumulate enough money to pay the port fee and choose to pay it
Ending B — Stay	Win	Reach the money threshold and decline to leave, or build deep enough NPC relationships and choose to remain
NPC departure (soft fail)	Soft fail	Ignore an NPC across multiple nights until their relationship meter hits zero
Soft fail recovery	Recovery	Leave a specific dish on the counter overnight as an offering to re-engage a departed NPC

9. Day / Night Cycle
•	Day phase: diner is closed. Player can cook, examine the corkboard, review savings. No time pressure.
•	Night phase: diner opens. NPCs arrive on their own schedule. Ends when the last NPC leaves.
•	Weather (fog, rain) changes daily and affects which NPCs appear.
•	Advancing the day triggers overnight recipe completions and stale item checks.

10. Corkboard System
NPCs occasionally leave 2D illustrated items on the counter — photographs, hand-drawn maps, torn notes, newspaper clippings. The player picks these up and pins them to a physical corkboard on the diner wall. Items can be examined up close by switching to a close-up camera. The corkboard fills gradually, telling the island’s story without cutscenes. Some items unlock late-game NPC dialogue or affect ending conditions.

